1:["$","div",null,{"className":"blog-shell","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"blog-header","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/","className":"wordmark","aria-label":"Wen Reloz, home","children":["wr",["$","span",null,{"children":"."}]]}],["$","nav",null,{"aria-label":"Primary navigation","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/","children":"PORTFOLIO"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"/"}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/blog","children":"BLOG"}]]}]]}],["$","main",null,{"className":"article-shell","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/blog","className":"back-link","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":[["path",{"d":"m12 19-7-7 7-7","key":"1l729n"}],["path",{"d":"M19 12H5","key":"x3x0zl"}]],"className":"lucide-arrow-left","size":15}]," All articles"]}],["$","article",null,{"children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"eyebrow","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"short-rule"}]," ","C# / PROBLEM & SOLUTION"]}],["$","h1",null,{"children":["The Request Succeeded Twice: Making a C# API Idempotent",["$","span",null,{"children":"."}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"article-lede","children":"A practical fix for the duplicate records that appear when a successful API request is retried."}],"$undefined",["$","div",null,{"className":"article-rule"}],[["$","div","The problem looked random: a user submitted one payment, but two payment records appeared. The endpoint was behaving correctly, the client had sent valid data, and neither request failed. The missing detail was that the same operation had reached the API twice.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"The problem looked random: a user submitted one payment, but two payment records appeared. The endpoint was behaving correctly, the client had sent valid data, and neither request failed. The missing detail was that the same operation had reached the API twice."}],false]}],["$","div","A request can finish on the server while its response is lost on the network. The client sees a timeout and retries. A person can double-click a button. A message consumer can receive the same event again. If every request means “insert a new row,” a retry becomes a second business operation.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"A request can finish on the server while its response is lost on the network. The client sees a timeout and retries. A person can double-click a button. A message consumer can receive the same event again. If every request means “insert a new row,” a retry becomes a second business operation."}],false]}],["$","div","Checking for an existing record before inserting seems like the obvious fix, but it leaves a race condition. Two requests can perform the check at the same time, both find nothing, and both insert. The database must enforce the rule because it is the only place that can arbitrate concurrent writes reliably.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"Checking for an existing record before inserting seems like the obvious fix, but it leaves a race condition. Two requests can perform the check at the same time, both find nothing, and both insert. The database must enforce the rule because it is the only place that can arbitrate concurrent writes reliably."}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"code-block","aria-label":"C# example","children":["$","code",null,{"children":"modelBuilder.Entity<ProcessedRequest>()\n    .HasIndex(x => x.IdempotencyKey)\n    .IsUnique();\n\npublic async Task<PaymentResult> CreateAsync(\n    CreatePayment command,\n    string idempotencyKey,\n    CancellationToken token)\n{\n    var saved = await db.ProcessedRequests\n        .SingleOrDefaultAsync(x => x.IdempotencyKey == idempotencyKey, token);\n\n    if (saved is not null)\n        return saved.ToPaymentResult();\n\n    var payment = Payment.Create(command);\n    db.Payments.Add(payment);\n    db.ProcessedRequests.Add(\n        ProcessedRequest.From(idempotencyKey, payment));\n\n    await db.SaveChangesAsync(token);\n    return PaymentResult.From(payment);\n}"}]}]]}],"$Le","$Lf","$L10","$L11","$L12"],"$L13"]}],"$L14"]}],"$L15"]}]
e:["$","div","I solve this by requiring an idempotency key for operations that must happen once. The client creates one key for the logical action and reuses it for every retry. The API stores that key with the result, and a unique database index guarantees that only one request can claim it.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"I solve this by requiring an idempotency key for operations that must happen once. The client creates one key for the logical action and reuses it for every retry. The API stores that key with the result, and a unique database index guarantees that only one request can claim it."}],false]}]
f:["$","div","The C# service first looks for a completed operation with the same key. If it exists, the service returns the saved result. Otherwise it creates the payment and its idempotency record in one SaveChanges call. EF Core applies that call as a transaction when the provider supports transactions, so the business change and stored response succeed or fail together.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"The C# service first looks for a completed operation with the same key. If it exists, the service returns the saved result. Otherwise it creates the payment and its idempotency record in one SaveChanges call. EF Core applies that call as a transaction when the provider supports transactions, so the business change and stored response succeed or fail together."}],false]}]
10:["$","div","The early lookup improves the normal retry path, but the unique constraint is the real protection. If two requests race, one insert succeeds and the other receives the provider’s unique-constraint error. The losing request can clear its tracked insert, read the committed result, and return the same response. In production code I identify that specific database error rather than treating every DbUpdateException as a duplicate.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"The early lookup improves the normal retry path, but the unique constraint is the real protection. If two requests race, one insert succeeds and the other receives the provider’s unique-constraint error. The losing request can clear its tracked insert, read the committed result, and return the same response. In production code I identify that specific database error rather than treating every DbUpdateException as a duplicate."}],false]}]
11:["$","div","This also changes the API contract. The key needs a defined scope and lifetime, and reusing it with a different payload should return a conflict instead of silently replaying an unrelated result. Old keys need a retention policy. Downstream side effects, such as publishing an event, need the same guarantee—often through an outbox written in the same transaction.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"This also changes the API contract. The key needs a defined scope and lifetime, and reusing it with a different payload should return a conflict instead of silently replaying an unrelated result. Old keys need a retention policy. Downstream side effects, such as publishing an event, need the same guarantee—often through an outbox written in the same transaction."}],false]}]
12:["$","div","The bug was never really “the user clicked twice.” It was an API that assumed delivery happens exactly once. Networks and queues do not make that promise. Idempotency turns retries from a source of duplicate work into a safe recovery mechanism.",{"children":[["$","p",null,{"children":"The bug was never really “the user clicked twice.” It was an API that assumed delivery happens exactly once. Networks and queues do not make that promise. Idempotency turns retries from a source of duplicate work into a safe recovery mechanism."}],false]}]
